\section*{Superreflexivity from nontrivial Markov type}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 327; 2026-09-23), the asserted result is that Markov type with exponent $p>1$ on a real Banach space forces superreflexivity and hence the existence of an equivalent uniformly smooth norm.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 327; 2026-09-23), a nonsuperreflexive space supplies a finitely represented spreading norm where adjacent equal-sign coefficients merge contractively. Reversible finite charts encode ordered motion with two separated levels, producing a walk that violates any nontrivial Markov-type bound.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 327; 2026-09-23).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 327; 2026-09-23), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: Markov type with exponent $p>1$ on a real Banach space forces superreflexivity and hence the existence of an equivalent uniformly smooth norm.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{MarkovType.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.MarkovSuperreflexivity.hasNontrivialMarkovType\_iff\_hasEquivalentUCNorm}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

